B-0 (Boosty) audit round 1, F1 + F6 (+ round 2, F1) -- six libc functions stop
reporting uninitialised stack, and sysconf's /ctl reads stop being
cancellation points.

0034 fixed sysconf(_SC_PHYS_PAGES): upstream called a sentinel-parked wrapper
(__lsysinfo) without checking it and computed from the struct it never filled.
The audit round (Fable 5.1) found the SAME defect sixty lines up in the SAME
function, and four more of the same shape. Each calls a wrapper whose syscall
is the 0xFFFF ENOSYS sentinel, ignores the failure, and reads the out-struct:

  sysconf(_SC_OPEN_MAX / _SC_CHILD_MAX)  getrlimit() unchecked -> stack residue
                                         clamped to LONG_MAX, errno clobbered
  getloadavg()      sysinfo() unchecked -> returns n "samples" of garbage, as
                    SUCCESS (GNU make -l N throttled on them; job.c:2138)
  getdtablesize()   getrlimit() unchecked -> garbage
  ulimit()          getrlimit() unchecked -> garbage
  getdomainname()   uname() unchecked -> strlen/strcpy over an uninitialised
                    struct utsname
  ualarm()          setitimer() unchecked -> "time left on the previous alarm"
                    read from an it_old nobody wrote. ROUND 2 found this one:
                    round 1's list said "alarm/ualarm return 0", true of
                    alarm() only because upstream wrote `old = { 0 }` there.

Why my own sweep missed them, recorded because the method is what gets reused:
I grepped the patched tree for statement-position `__syscall(`. These are
unchecked WRAPPER calls -- which is exactly what 0034's bug was. The sweep that
finds them is "every libc-internal call to a wrapper over a sentinel whose
return value is ignored". Round 2 ran exactly that over the whole patched src/
(a named-wrapper pass and a generic pass over the 213 always-failing wrappers):
the only out-struct readers left were ualarm.c and alarm.c.

Answers, each the honest one its API allows:
  sysconf       -1 with errno as the caller left it (POSIX: indeterminate)
  getloadavg    -1 (its documented failure)
  ulimit        -1
  getdomainname -1 (uname already set errno)
  ualarm        -1 with the wrapper's ENOSYS (glibc's answer when the timer
                cannot be set), and it_old is zero-initialised
  getdtablesize 1024 -- the one MIRROR here. The API has no error channel,
                INT_MAX would turn every close-all-fds loop into a hang, and
                the kernel's per-Proc handle table is PROC_HANDLE_MAX = 1024.
                No file exposes that figure, so the prover MEASURES it:
                pouch-hello-malloc opens /ctl/memory until the kernel
                refuses and requires getdtablesize() to equal the last fd
                issued plus one (the table fills lowest-free-first).

F6: sysconf's two /ctl arms (0032 nprocs, 0034 phys pages) read and closed
their fd with read()/close(), which are cancellation points; sysconf is not
one. A cancel pending at the read leaked the fd. Both arms now use raw
__syscall(SYS_read / SYS_close). open() on pouch is already non-cancellable.
The 0032 arm also left open()'s errno behind on its fail-soft return of 1 (a
call that did not fail reporting ENOENT), and its comment still said /ctl is
absent pre-pivot, which stopped being true at #57a; both corrected here.

Device-side pin (same commit, not in this patch file): pouch-hello-malloc
pre-loads errno with a value none of these calls sets and requires
sysconf(_SC_OPEN_MAX) and sysconf(_SC_CHILD_MAX) to return -1 with it
untouched, getloadavg / ulimit(UL_GETFSIZE) / getdomainname to return -1 (the
last with errno == ENOSYS: on the unfixed libc it also returned -1 whenever the
stack residue held no NUL, but with EINVAL), ualarm() to return (unsigned)-1
with ENOSYS, and getdtablesize() to match the measured table.

